  "theory": {
    "title": "Theory",
    "inspect": "Mean and variance describe brightness and spread. Entropy measures how many bits per pixel the histogram needs. A flat histogram means high entropy.",
    "point": "Point operations map each input level to an output level with a lookup table. Equalization uses the cumulative histogram; gamma and log curves compress or expand tones without spatial filtering.",
    "sampling": "Quantization reduces the number of gray levels to 2^bits; nearest-neighbor downsampling keeps one source pixel per output pixel and can cause aliasing.",
    "spatial": "A spatial filter replaces each pixel with a weighted sum of its neighborhood. Smoothing kernels average noise; sharpen and unsharp add high-frequency detail; median rejects outliers; gradient operators estimate edges.",
    "frequency": "The 2D Fourier transform maps an image to spatial frequencies. Lowpass filters keep smooth structure, highpass keeps edges, bandpass keeps oriented texture, and notch filters suppress specific periodic noise."
  }
